1
00:00:01,004 --> 00:00:04,252
(Intro music)

2
00:00:04,309 --> 00:00:08,542
NASA ASTROPHYSICS

3
00:00:09,471 --> 00:00:10,940
(Rhythmic cosmic music)

4
00:00:10,986 --> 00:00:15,569
Narrator: Eta Carinae is the most
massive star within 10,000 light-years.

5
00:00:16,485 --> 00:00:19,531
In the mid-nineteenth century,
a tremendous eruption

6
00:00:19,575 --> 00:00:23,449
hurled enough gas into space
to make at least 10 copies of the sun,

7
00:00:23,495 --> 00:00:26,531
creating an expanding nebula
still visible today.

8
00:00:26,748 --> 00:00:30,275
New results from NASA satellites,
ground-based telescopes,

9
00:00:30,291 --> 00:00:33,181
and theoretical models
are providing astronomers

10
00:00:33,202 --> 00:00:35,768
with an improved
understanding of the system.

11
00:00:35,825 --> 00:00:38,931
At the nebula's heart lie two giant stars.

12
00:00:39,199 --> 00:00:42,192
The smaller of the two
is 30 times the mass of the sun

13
00:00:42,233 --> 00:00:44,089
and a million times brighter.

14
00:00:44,251 --> 00:00:48,976
The primary weighs 90 suns and shines
5 times brighter than its companion.

15
00:00:49,242 --> 00:00:53,284
Both stars produce powerful outflows
called stellar winds,

16
00:00:53,302 --> 00:00:55,075
but the primary's is so dense

17
00:00:55,087 --> 00:00:58,253
it prevents astronomers
from directly observing the star.

18
00:00:59,060 --> 00:01:02,275
The secondary's orbit is among
the most eccentric known.

19
00:01:02,488 --> 00:01:04,145
Every five and a half years,

20
00:01:04,176 --> 00:01:07,790
when the two stars swing
toward closest approach, or periastron,

21
00:01:07,819 --> 00:01:10,938
the come about as close
to each other as Mars is from the sun.

22
00:01:12,029 --> 00:01:16,226
Eta Carinae's most recent periastron
occurred in August 2014,

23
00:01:16,272 --> 00:01:19,051
and many of NASA's space-based telescopes,

24
00:01:19,090 --> 00:01:22,029
as well as ground-based
observatories, looked on.

25
00:01:22,812 --> 00:01:24,446
Where the stellar winds collide,

26
00:01:24,491 --> 00:01:27,024
temperatures reach
hundreds of millions degrees -

27
00:01:27,069 --> 00:01:29,203
hot enough to emit X-rays.

28
00:01:29,437 --> 00:01:32,544
But the X-ray emission
shuts down near periastron,

29
00:01:32,557 --> 00:01:36,699
when the smaller star is swallowed up
by the dense wind of its larger companion.

30
00:01:37,802 --> 00:01:41,021
The X-rays gradually recover
after periastron,

31
00:01:41,036 --> 00:01:43,449
but they do so
a bit differently each time,

32
00:01:43,506 --> 00:01:46,534
suggesting structural changes
in the dueling winds.

33
00:01:47,078 --> 00:01:51,894
Recent observations have helped theorists
refine computer models of this interaction.

34
00:01:52,218 --> 00:01:56,735
Here, darker colors indicate
lower gas densities around the stars,

35
00:01:56,778 --> 00:01:59,860
while the stars themselves
appear as black circles.

36
00:02:00,010 --> 00:02:03,166
When the stars are far apart,
the winds collide head-on,

37
00:02:03,209 --> 00:02:07,014
creating a huge cavity surrounded
by 50-million-degree gas.

38
00:02:07,204 --> 00:02:11,080
But near periastron, the secondary
whips around the larger star,

39
00:02:11,126 --> 00:02:13,664
carving a tunnel
though the primary's dense wind.

40
00:02:13,932 --> 00:02:18,976
The secondary star's rapid motion changes
the collision zone from head... to tail.

41
00:02:19,673 --> 00:02:23,471
The smaller star's faster wind flows out
along the cavity it created

42
00:02:23,518 --> 00:02:26,762
and then crashes into
the outward-moving wind of its companion.

43
00:02:27,815 --> 00:02:30,782
From out perspective on Earth,
the X-rays fade,

44
00:02:30,801 --> 00:02:33,135
then recover, as the stars move apart.

45
00:02:33,818 --> 00:02:36,545
New 3D models of these simulations reveal

46
00:02:36,562 --> 00:02:40,968
the formation of finger-like protrusions
along the cavity near periastron,

47
00:02:40,999 --> 00:02:43,098
features never before identified.

48
00:02:43,481 --> 00:02:48,214
Theorists now investigating the phenomenon
suggest they may arise from instabilities

49
00:02:48,246 --> 00:02:51,335
developing in gas
as it flows along the cavity wall.

50
00:02:52,213 --> 00:02:54,680
Astronomers also keep an eye
on Eta Carinae

51
00:02:54,707 --> 00:02:56,566
with the Hubble Space Telescope.

52
00:02:57,529 --> 00:03:00,190
These images show a structure
resembling a crab,

53
00:03:00,219 --> 00:03:04,299
formed by gas excited
by the secondary star's intense UV light.

54
00:03:05,038 --> 00:03:07,240
This light dims near periastron,

55
00:03:07,270 --> 00:03:10,744
when the secondary passes through
the thickest part of the primary's wind.

56
00:03:10,772 --> 00:03:14,063
The gas relaxes, settling into
a lower-energy state.

57
00:03:15,006 --> 00:03:18,470
As a result, the crab appears
in a different spectral line

58
00:03:18,516 --> 00:03:21,686
until the stars move apart
and the UV light is restored.

59
00:03:23,051 --> 00:03:27,413
Eta Carinae is one of the closest
and most massive colliding-wind binaries,

60
00:03:27,480 --> 00:03:29,505
and serves as a natural laboratory

61
00:03:29,535 --> 00:03:32,107
for studying these rare
and important objects.

62
00:03:32,901 --> 00:03:35,534
While its past eruptions
remain unexplained,

63
00:03:35,586 --> 00:03:38,498
astronomers think they have
a handle on its current state--

64
00:03:38,527 --> 00:03:43,002
a claim to be tested
at the next periastron in February 2020.

65
00:03:43,277 --> 00:03:45,746
(Music fades)

66
00:03:47,742 --> 00:03:51,563
(Beeping)